			<div class="content" id="post-content-1453504">
									

<p>I just don't like this one. In essence, it's the old man, but radioactive and teddybear.</p>
<p>For one, the containment procedures seem needlessly extravagant&#8230;. you know that a smaller glass enclosure would probably mean less phosphorus needed to achieve the illumination desired.. besides, why not have a vial with phosphorus within 4m near it, replace it weekly, and have the thing illuminated by arc lamps on several fail-safe circuits?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-1145 is radioactive, with doses measured between 300 and █████ mSv/h</p>
</blockquote>
<p>No. Dose is a term and Sievert an unit relating to <em>absorbtion</em> of radiation. Becquerel is the unit of emission - 1&#160;Bq = one nucleus per second decay rate.<br />
Hell, there are other units that'd make sense ,but not what you used.</p>
<p>Also, where the fuck is the phosphor? On the inside of the glass block? You know that glass is enough to stop beta radiation, right? Or do the D-class have to paint it from inside?</p>

